(vector (map (:db/id -1)
  (:adr/id "ADR-2608196800")
  (:adr/title "a detector that refused to answer was recorded as clean, and marked 337 findings resolved")
  (:adr/status "accepted")
  (:adr/date "2026-08-19")
  (:adr/tags "detectors evidence-floor exit-codes registry co-scientist")
  (:adr/body
  "## The failure

`verify-appview-page-summary` did everything this workspace asks of a check. It
has an evidence floor, it refuses rather than guessing, it exits 2, and it says
why:

    CANNOT ANSWER: found 142 candidate pages, floor 150. Reporting `no page
    disagrees` from almost no input is the failure this floor exists to prevent.

The registry recorded that run as:

    verify-appview-page-summary → ok exit 2 1s 0 finding(s) scanned=100
      RESOLVED  page-summary-route-count:orgs/cloud-itonami/aidesk
      RESOLVED  page-summary-route-count:orgs/cloud-itonami/air-book
      … 337 of them

**A refusal was read as a clean sweep**, and 337 standing findings -- landing pages
telling visitors they have no public route, at an address wrangler declares -- were
marked fixed.

## Why the existing protection did not fire

The tick already guards against this one branch below, with the right comment:

    ;; An :inconclusive or :error run must NOT overwrite the finding set.
    ;; Otherwise a detector that failed to start would report every standing
    ;; finding as RESOLVED, which is the most dangerous possible lie this
    ;; thing could tell.

What was missing was **reaching** it. `run-one` mapped every non-killed exit to
`:ok` (correct for detectors that exit 1 when they find things), and `qualify`
downgrades only when the evidence regex fails to match. A detector with a floor
**prints its evidence line before refusing** -- it has to, the count is the reason
it is refusing -- so `SCANNED	100` matched, the status stayed `:ok`, and the
merge treated an empty finding list as a repaired fleet.

The protection was written against *a detector that failed to start*. The case it
missed is *a detector that started, worked correctly, and declined to answer* --
which is the more careful detector, punished for being careful.

## Change

`run-one` classifies by exit code: **0 = clean, 1 = found things, >=2 = could not
answer**. Measured 2026-08-19 across all 15 registered detectors: every one exits
0 or 1, and several state the three-valued convention in their own headers
(`verify-app-content-address`, `verify-source-path-pins`, `verify-kip-quorum`,
`verify-shadow-source-paths`). Nothing in the registry has to change.

Both directions, same tick, on copies of the live state:

    before:  → ok           exit 2, 0 findings   -> findings 0, resolved 337
    after:   → inconclusive exit 2, 0 findings   -> findings 337, resolved 0
    control: verify-layer-deps           exit 0  -> ok, 6 findings, merged
             verify-worker-name-collisions exit 1 -> ok, 8 findings, merged

The SessionStart hook already prints a ⚠ line for any status that is not `:ok`, so
a refusing detector is visible rather than silently absent.

## Open, measured, deliberately not fixed here

The refusal itself is real: candidates fell from ~164 (baseline, 17 h earlier) to
**142**, below the floor of 150. **Do not lower the floor to make the check
answerable** -- that is the instinct this floor exists to defeat. The question to
answer first is why the input shrank: 422 `+page.svelte` files exist on disk, the
detector's candidate filter selects 142, and another session has been advancing
west pins throughout the day. Repaired pages, renamed repositories and stale
checkouts all look alike from here.

## The shape, again

This is the fifth consecutive iteration of one defect: **something that could not
measure produced the same output as something that measured and found nothing.**
The new detail is where it lived. The detector was right, its floor was right, its
exit code was right, and the machinery that files its answer discarded all three.
It is not enough for a check to refuse; whatever records the refusal has to be able
to hear it.")))
